WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.360
What is the degree awarding gap?

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.760
In higher education,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.120
a degree awarding gap
refers to the difference

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.760
between the proportion of students
from one group awarded a first class

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.760
or upper second degree compared
with another peer group.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.960
The widest gaps are between
the groupings of disabled and non-disabled

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:30.000
higher and lower socioeconomic and white
and black and minority ethnic students.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.040
The largest awarding gap currently exists
between black and white student peers.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.560
Even when a student's age, gender,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.120
socioeconomic status
and entry qualifications are similar.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.000
The degree awarding gap still exists,
making this a concern for institutions.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.520
In England, the Office for Students, part
of the Department for Education, states

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.520
that the differences in degree
award outcomes may be the consequence

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.280
of a lack of diverse environments
and a non-inclusive curriculum.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.800
Degree
awarding gaps become more pronounced

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.800
if students belong to more than one
category, like being a woman

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.200
from a low socioeconomic background
and being from an ethnic minority.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.160
This is the impact of intersectionality.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.400
Historically, universities have adopted
what is known as a students

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.640
deficit model,
blaming the individual for academic

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.640
weakness,
or perhaps choosing the wrong course.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:30.440
This lack of achievement was entitled
The Attainment Gap.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.360
The contemporary term awarding gap
rather than viewing the students

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.440
as deficient, places the onus on
the institution to review, consider

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.560
and action their policies and practices
as to why the awarding gaps persist.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.800
What are the causes?

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.680
There are many complex reasons
behind the gaps between distinct groups.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.520
A higher Education
funding Council for England report

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.680
suggested the limitations of teacher
expectations

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.880
of students
as having an impact on grade outcomes.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.680
However, focusing on the largest awarding
gap between whites in black and minority

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:13.200
ethnic students, some of the causes
suggested by Universities UK

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.760
and the National Union of Students
include institutional culture.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.880
The culture of an institution
is identified

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.840
as a crucial factor in relation
to the degrees awarded to students

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.440
from Black, Asian and minority
ethnic backgrounds.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.480
Ethnic
diversity among role models and staff.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.520
Having small numbers of black,

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.440
Asian and minority ethnic staff
has been acknowledged as limiting

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.960
and institutions capability
to address the awarding gaps.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.960
Curriculum contents, design and delivery.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.600
The design of the curriculum,
its contents, the way it's written

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.800
and how it's taught
can have a significant impact

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.400
on the way that students from diverse
backgrounds and ethnicities

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.640
respond to their course, lecturers
and tutors.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.320
Belonging.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.240
A strong theme in literature is
the importance of inclusion and belonging.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.240
The National Union of Students
found that when black,

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.280
Asian and minority
ethnic students were asked

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:20.160
why they were less likely to be satisfied
with their experience

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.680
and felt less likely
to get a first or upper second respondents

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:30.600
repeatedly cited feelings of discomfort,
isolation and a sense of not belonging.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.680
Social, cultural and economic capital.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.360
Access to advice, guidance and resources
and finance can depend on

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:41.880
whether a student's parents

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.240
or guardians have the knowledge
and experience to support them.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.480
These obstacles can be made worse
by schools and colleges,

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.280
not providing sufficient information
or guidance resources.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.280
How do we reduce the awarding gap?

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:58.240
AdvanceHE

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.920
recommends higher education institutions
should adopt a project approach

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.560
which focuses on three key elements
to close the awarding gaps.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.280
Organizational change,
improving institutional processes,

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.200
enhancing the knowledge
and skills of staff and better supporting

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.840
students are important to address
unequal outcomes for students.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.920
Institutions can do this through
developing mentoring schemes or workshops,

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.760
embedding equality, diversity
and inclusion in promotion criteria,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.000
and measuring the impact of contemporary
initiatives.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.240
Inclusive Curriculum.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.600
An inclusive curriculum is one
that is student centered

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.760
and allows students to draw
on their own experiences and prospects

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:48.000
so that students are leading the learning
and are learning from each other.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:52.880
It includes multi-sensory
delivery strategies which expose students

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.600
to a variety of mediums that are exciting,
engaging and interactive,

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.880
inviting speakers from different cultural
and social backgrounds

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:05.560
to cover additional aspects of the
curriculum or different perspectives

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.560
also enriches the curriculum.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.840
Student Support.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.320
Student support services are a key area

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.440
for addressing black and minority
ethnic awarding gaps.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.160
There are many examples of student support
activities,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.680
student co-creation training,
student partnership and mentoring schemes,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.680
Student course representative training
and partnering with a student union.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.040
Degree Awarding
gaps can have a lasting impact

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.120
on people's
lives, families and communities.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.680
Many companies and postgraduate courses
require a first class

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.240
or upper second degree for graduates
Employment or study,

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:49.200
so the gap can limit people's life,
chances, opportunities and incomes.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.800
By working to close degree awarding gaps,

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.160
we are creating equitable
outcomes for all students.

